<HEAD><style>/* Classic Editorial — identity is hierarchy, spacing, grid, and proportion. Every value below
   is a theme variable; nothing depends on a face, a hue, or a texture. */
.gml-card {
  --hairline: var(--neutral-border-color);
  --grid-divider-color: var(--hairline);
  --grid-divider-width: 1px;
}

/* 1 · Kicker over a hairline: the tracked-caps eyebrow carries its own full-width hairline as a
   bottom border, so the pair is one node and the heading below keeps the native eyebrow hug.
   Dose: one per card, top of the column it introduces. */
.kicker.gml-paragraph { padding-bottom: 7pt; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); margin-block-end: 10pt; }
/* Heavy rule: the masthead / act-break register in heading ink. Dose: covers, dividers, closing. */
.heavy.gml-divider { --divider-color: var(--heading-color); --divider-thickness: 2pt; border-radius: 0; }

/* 2 · Stat rail and metric: tabular figures so stacked numbers align; the gloss hugs its figure. */
.metric { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums; }
.metric h2.gml-heading, .metric h3.gml-heading, .metric .gml-title { letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
.gloss.gml-paragraph { margin-block-start: calc(var(--block-gap) * 0.3); }
/* Inside a metric rail the gloss sits directly under its figure: the figure's own descent supplies the air. */
.metric .gloss.gml-paragraph { margin-block-start: 0; }

/* Column takeaway under a metric rail: the concluding claim stands off from the last ruled row by
   more than the rows keep between themselves, so it reads as the column's conclusion rather than a fourth figure. */
.claim.gml-paragraph { margin-block-start: calc(var(--block-gap) * 2.5); }

/* 3 · Two-column measure: sustained prose at 55–70 characters per line, ragged right, no seam. */
.prose p.gml-paragraph { text-wrap: pretty; hyphens: manual; }
.measure { max-width: 520pt; }
/* Masthead headline (cover, closing): nine columns, so a 60-character headline wraps to two lines. */
.measure.wide { max-width: 660pt; text-wrap: balance; }

/* 5 · Accent quote glyph and quote voice: one oversized opening mark set as a display node beside
   the quote; the quote itself reads in the heading voice with no bar. */
.quote.gml-title { line-height: 0.6; margin-block-end: 0; }
.quote.gml-blockquote { padding-inline-start: 0; }
.quote.gml-blockquote > .gml-blockquote__bar { display: none; }
.quote.gml-blockquote p.gml-paragraph { font-family: var(--heading-font); font-weight: var(--heading-font-weight); line-height: 1.12; letter-spacing: -0.015em; }

/* 6 · Figure caption grammar: a semibold lead-in, a muted sentence, then a credit/source line. */
.source.gml-paragraph { margin-block-start: calc(var(--block-gap) * 0.45); }

/* 7 · Ruled ledger: caps header over a heavy rule, hairlines between rows, numerals right-set. */
.ledger { --table-border-color: var(--hairline); --table-border-width: 1px; --table-header-color: var(--heading-color); --table-header-weight: 600; --table-cell-padding: 12pt 12pt 12pt 0; --table-radius: 0; }
.ledger .gml-table__row[data-header] .gml-table__cell { border-bottom: 2pt solid var(--heading-color); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: calc(11 * var(--type-anchor, 1pt)); }
.ledger .gml-table__cell:not([data-col='0']) { text-align: end; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.ledger .gml-table__row:last-child .gml-table__cell { border-bottom: var(--table-border-width) solid var(--table-border-color); }

/* 8 · Numbered column head: a light large numeral over a hairline, drawn by the grid marker so it
   renumbers on reorder. Dose: peer columns and steps. */
.tick .gml-grid-cell { --marker-color: var(--heading-color); --marker-font-weight: 300; --marker-font-size: calc(var(--role-display-md-pt, 52) * var(--type-anchor, 1pt)); --marker-rule-color: var(--hairline); --marker-rule-thickness: 1px; }

/* Contents rows: muted inline ordinals in a narrow gutter. */
.toc .gml-grid-cell { --marker-color: var(--body-color-muted); --marker-font-weight: 400; --marker-inline-size: 30pt; --marker-gap: 10pt; }

/* Photographic plates keep square corners — the register has no rounded frames. A caption or name
   set under an in-flow plate keeps a clear line of air from the crop (≥14pt). */
.plate .gml-figure, .plate .gml-figure__img { border-radius: 0; }
.plate .gloss.gml-paragraph, .plate h4.gml-heading { margin-block-start: 15pt; }
/* People cells: the role line hugs its name (6pt) and the credit sentence stands off from the role (16pt),
   so name+role read as one group and the sentence as the next. */
.plate .meta.gml-paragraph { margin-block-start: -5pt; margin-block-end: 7pt; }

/* Cover metadata (byline, date, folio-style credits): tracked caps at caption size. */
.cover-meta.gml-paragraph { letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }

/* Icon chips on the icon-led list: one outlined circle treatment, an outlined circle over each column head. */
.chips .gml-grid-cell { --marker-border-color: var(--heading-color); --marker-border-width: 1px; --marker-color: var(--heading-color); --marker-gap: 10pt; }</style></HEAD>
<section image-layout="blank" id="n-xNJR73Z8"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="OL90RTLHuE"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">VISUAL COMMUNICATION HISTORY · THREE MEDIA REGIMES</p><h1 class="measure wide">The Module, the Rule, the Model: Visual Transformation Across Three Media Regimes</h1><h3 role="subheading" class="measure">How design moved from arranging manufactured parts, to specifying executable systems, to judging outputs produced by statistical models.</h3><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="caption" class="cover-meta">Print civilization · Digital era · Computational intelligence</p><hr class="heavy"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="RVgTcAUBvX"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="w1gQuaka70"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">THE ARGUMENT</p><h2>Every media revolution moves the designer one step further from the artifact</h2><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill" class="tick"><grid-item id="O-y7oIJTN_" colspan="20"><h3>The module</h3><p>European printers industrialized a letter cast once and reused everywhere. That repeatable part reorganized production and the book trade.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="69ZE6US28L" colspan="20"><h3>The rule</h3><p>Swiss modernism and desktop publishing made layout a system that could generate pages the designer did not draw individually.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="8UoXNgjHKs" colspan="20"><h3>The model</h3><p>Generative systems shift the deliverable toward specified intent, followed by selection and rejection of outputs nobody directly composed.</p></grid-item></grid><p class="claim"><b>The claim to test:</b> each shift traded direct control for reach, and each time the discipline argued about what was lost.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="AMJNCsYppR"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="fB2mETtpCg"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">A TEACHING CHRONOLOGY</p><h2>Five dates that reorganized the discipline</h2><p role="caption" class="gloss">These are European and industrial markers for the lecture’s argument. 1455 marks European adoption, not the origin of printing.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><smart-layout variant="minimalTimeline" orientation="horizontal" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>1455</h4><p>Mainz: metal type scales into an industry</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>1928</h4><p>Tschichold: the page becomes a system</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>1964</h4><p>Gerstner: design becomes a programme</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>1985</h4><p>PageMaker: trades collapse into one desk</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>2016</h4><p>Variable fonts: type becomes a continuous space</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="caption" class="source">Sources: <a href="https://guides.loc.gov/gutenberg/introduction">Library of Congress</a> · <a href="https://www.lars-mueller-publishers.com/designing-programmes">Lars Müller Publishers</a> · <a href="https://www.historyofinformation.com/detail.php?entryid=1235">History of Information</a> · <a href="https://opensource.googleblog.com/2016/09/introducing-opentype-font-variations.html">Google Open Source</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="OCE9hJ9wVt"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="OQ66EaNIwE"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">PART ONE</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><display size="lg">I</display><h1 class="measure">European metal type: the letter as a manufactured part</h1><p role="caption" class="gloss">The module scales production, stabilizes the page and changes what a book can do.</p><hr class="heavy"><spacer mode="fill"></spacer></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="6FAWZCjvwv"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="OeQ82JoIx1"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">ORIGIN AND ADOPTION</p><h2>Mainz industrialized the module; it did not invent it</h2><table col-widths="[16,84]" borders="rows" striped="false"><tbody><tr><td><h4>11 May 868</h4></td><td><p>The Chinese woodblock <i>Diamond Sutra</i> from Dunhuang is the earliest dated printed book.</p></td></tr><tr><td><h4>1041–1048</h4></td><td><p>Bi Sheng cut individual baked-clay characters, an invention recorded by Shen Kuo.</p></td></tr><tr><td><h4>13th century</h4></td><td><p>Cast-metal type was in use in Korea.</p></td></tr><tr><td><h4>July 1377</h4></td><td><p>The <i>Jikji</i>, printed at Heungdeoksa, is the oldest surviving metal-type book, 78 years before the 42-line Bible.</p></td></tr><tr><td><h4>1455</h4></td><td><p>Gutenberg’s workshop combined cast metal sorts, oily ink and the press for a large European edition.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p class="claim"><b>This lecture’s reading:</b> what spread from Mainz was industrial adoption. The visual consequences follow from that scale.</p><p role="caption" class="source">Sources: <a href="https://idp.bl.uk/discover/learning/dunhuang/collection-items/cave-17-the-library-cave/">British Library</a> · <a href="https://www.historyofinformation.com/detail.php?id=19">History of Information</a> · <a href="https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/songdynasty-module/tech-printing.html">Columbia University</a> · <a href="https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/baegun-hwasang-chorok-buljo-jikji-simche-yojeol-volii-second-volume-anthology-great-buddhist-priests?hub=1081">UNESCO</a> · <a href="https://guides.loc.gov/gutenberg/introduction">Library of Congress</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="vEZN78Cwlt"><grid divider="sm neutral-border"><grid-item id="LZlwjAf-Vx" class="prose" colspan="36"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">PRODUCTION AS DESIGN</p><h2>The 42-line Bible was a factory imitating a scriptorium</h2><p>Production took about <b>three years</b>. Scholars read pages set twice as evidence of a mid-production decision to enlarge the run, an increase estimated near 30%.</p><p>Visually, the book imitated German manuscript hands: dense black textura, narrow columns and hand-finished initials.</p><p class="claim">A new medium’s first masterpiece is usually a facsimile of the thing it displaces.</p><p role="caption" class="source">Sources: <a href="https://specialcollections-blog.lib.cam.ac.uk/?p=13354">Cambridge University Library</a> · <a href="https://webs.uab.cat/aurelialabert/wp-content/uploads/sites/108/2024/08/34.Rangel-Alabert2022JPHS3.pdf">Rangel and Alabert</a> · <a href="https://guides.loc.gov/gutenberg/introduction">Library of Congress</a></p></grid-item><grid-item id="1YZx9vt7VI" colspan="24"><grid divider="sm neutral-border" direction="vertical" class="metric"><grid-item id="92ZrX4mP1S"><h2><span text-color="accent">16,000</span></h2><p role="caption" class="gloss">roughly this many pieces of type</p></grid-item><grid-item id="y29VX_xg1K"><h2>50,000</h2><p role="caption" class="gloss">nearly this many sheets of paper</p></grid-item><grid-item id="w-fQRY2JjU"><h2>180</h2><p role="caption" class="gloss">around this many copies, some 45 on parchment</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="rSlGXrzHJy"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="9iqpyfYU75"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">TYPOGRAPHIC FIXITY</p><h2>The page became an interface</h2><p class="measure">Near-identical copies made stable reference possible. Across the century after 1455, European printed books acquired a navigational kit that readers could learn once and reuse.</p><grid spacing="small" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="rj6uwsTq9T" border="lg solid" border-sides="left" colspan="20"><h4>Title page</h4><p>Identifies the edition.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="u1NQnSOrU1" border="lg solid" border-sides="left" colspan="20"><h4>Page numbers</h4><p>Give every passage a location.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="29U8oMrP6y" border="lg solid" border-sides="left" colspan="20"><h4>Running heads</h4><p>Keep context visible.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="47fXODtWmV" border="lg solid" border-sides="left" colspan="20"><h4>Contents</h4><p>Maps the document.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="e7XNNZSkjj" border="lg solid" border-sides="left" colspan="20"><h4>Index</h4><p>Turns subjects into routes.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="vLjyt30WEh" border="lg solid" border-sides="left" colspan="20"><h4>Footnotes</h4><p>Attach evidence and dispute.</p></grid-item></grid><p class="claim">Elizabeth Eisenstein called this <b>typographic fixity</b>: the argument that stable text can be cited, corrected and disputed across cities and generations. Standardization becomes a precondition for comparison.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="q4mRgxgIec"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="uVf-8zddmZ"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">SYSTEM AND IDEOLOGY</p><h2>The grid: a moral argument disguised as a layout tool</h2><grid divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="8FKGCkVwf0" background-color="surface" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">THE CLAIM</p><h3>Order could be objective</h3><p>Tschichold’s <i>Die neue Typographie</i>, 1928, framed asymmetry, sans serif and the flush-left rag as an ethical position against ornament and nationalism.</p><p>Müller-Brockmann’s <i>Grid Systems</i>, 1981, presented the grid as order and the designer as neutral servant of the message.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="p3Ij1g1xQV" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">THE CRITIQUE</p><h3>Neutrality has a history</h3><p>The modernist promise was that identical rules applied to any content would yield clarity independent of taste.</p><p>Yet neutrality is itself a style. The Swiss grid is a mid-century European aesthetic, not a universal law of perception.</p></grid-item></grid><p class="claim">The structural change matters most: the designer authors a <b>system</b>, then delegates the individual page to it.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="B1NhU7fMzy"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="NtURDFuwAx"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">PROGRAMMATIC DESIGN</p><h2>Gerstner, 1964: designing programmes, before the tools existed</h2><p class="measure">Across four essays, Karl Gerstner proposed systematic design as a model at the dawn of the computer era. His decisive move was to design the <b>set of all acceptable solutions</b>, rather than one solution.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><grid marker="number" marker-size="large" marker-style="subtle" marker-shape="chevron" marker-position="above" marker-align="center" marker-fill="true" spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug" padding="1em" style="--marker-chip-padding: 0.75em;"><grid-item id="x0xQY1myRl" colspan="20"><h4>Define parameters</h4><p>State the variables and constraints.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="SvQh5wDIX7" colspan="20"><h4>Generate possibilities</h4><p>Describe a space of layouts.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="qcg2ZSKiuB" colspan="20"><h4>Select</h4><p>Choose one acceptable result.</p></grid-item></grid><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p>A parameter-driven identity or modular typeface is a programme, not a single layout. Lars Müller reissued the book because it now reads like a specification for computational design.</p><p role="caption" class="source">Source: <a href="https://www.lars-mueller-publishers.com/designing-programmes">Lars Müller Publishers, <i>Designing Programmes</i></a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="Jo556W8fRn"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="5q57p575lr"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">PART TWO</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><display size="lg">II</display><h1 class="measure">The digital era: the rule becomes executable</h1><p role="caption" class="gloss">The page turns into code, the trades converge and the artifact begins adapting to its context.</p><hr class="heavy"><spacer mode="fill"></spacer></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="NpJ-eryDlX"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="HTc2-vbnZb"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">DESKTOP PUBLISHING</p><h2>1985: three products collapse five centuries of trades</h2><p class="measure">Paul Brainerd called the new system a <b>three-legged stool</b>. Each product solved a different part of the workflow.</p><grid marker="icon" marker-size="large" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill" class="chips"><grid-item id="IoextfgDWs" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/65J17SqGzUfRnZ9L4BXm.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="early personal computer graphical interface" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A monitor symbolizes the concept of a computer, often used to represent technology and digital communication in a simple visual form.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Macintosh</h3><p>The 1984 computer made visual composition available at one desk.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="_vT9IC9qTE" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/7Ac18mbeFpXHbaq2eM8v.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="coded page sent to printer" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black rectangular shape with a protruding curved line on top, symbolizing the concept of a printer.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Adobe PostScript</h3><p>A page became resolution-independent code that could print anywhere.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="7xI7SFHuS5" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/7dvsbGkLYjsD3TgdVZ2d.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="page layout software document" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A rectangular shape with a flat top and bottom, and straight sides, often symbolizing a document or a sheet of paper in a digital context.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Aldus PageMaker</h3><p>Released July 1985, it assembled type and images on screen.</p></grid-item></grid><hr><h3>Compositor, typesetter, paste-up artist and platemaker became one person at one desk.</h3><p>Brainerd coined <i>desktop publishing</i> to name an industry that did not yet exist. Access expanded; real deskilling followed. Aldus borrowed the name of the Venetian printer Aldus Manutius, reaching for old legitimacy.</p><p role="caption" class="source">Sources: <a href="https://archive.computerhistory.org/resources/text/Oral_History/Brainerd_Paul/Brainerd_Paul_1.oral_history.2006.102657986.pdf">Computer History Museum</a> · <a href="https://www.historyofinformation.com/detail.php?entryid=1235">History of Information</a> · <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldus_Corporation">Aldus Corporation</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="J-FMn5Rfsa" card-margin-hidden="true"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="qfHdGEqR2W" class="plate" padding="0" colspan="28"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/19JnJaMF2gUWRzscDhOAj.png" saved-media-id="k9lxsq9ldlfe274" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="documentary-feature" alt="Documentary reportage photograph styled as an honest 1980s period reconstruction of a type designer working at an early Macintosh in a small independent magazine studio, coarse bitmap letterforms visible only as abstract pixel shapes on the monochrome screen, dot-matrix proofs and cut paper on the wall, fluorescent and window light mixed, candid standing-eye-level view, true-to-life neutral color, no logos or legible text" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"></grid-item><grid-item id="dUqCiBS8Nr" class="prose" padding="32pt 40pt 20pt" colspan="32"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">THE MACHINE’S GRAIN</p><h3>Emigre: designing with the machine’s grain</h3><p>Early screens and printers offered coarse pixel grids. Much of the profession read that grain as degradation.</p><p>Zuzana Licko drew bitmap faces that treated the pixel as the module. The constraint became the design language.</p><blockquote class="quote"><p font-size="22pt">You read best what you read most.</p></blockquote><p>Her line challenged the idea that legibility is fixed in forms rather than learned through habit. <i>Emigre</i> magazine made the argument a decade-long public quarrel about authorship and taste.</p><p class="claim">When a new medium looks ugly, ask whether you are seeing a defect or an unfamiliar grain.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="caption" class="source">Image: illustrative period reconstruction</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="HPZWIXd0ou"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="iH4kf45oEq"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">RESPONSIVE DESIGN</p><h2>2010: the page dissolves, and layout becomes a rule you cannot preview</h2><p>Ethan Marcotte published “Responsive Web Design” in <i>A List Apart</i> on 25 May 2010, after naming the idea in a talk that April. The method had three ingredients.</p><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="9xQFhOPesH" border="lg solid" border-sides="top" colspan="20"><h3>Fluid grids</h3><p>Proportions replace fixed page dimensions.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="pUlHumCAl4" border="lg solid" border-sides="top" colspan="20"><h3>Flexible images</h3><p>Media scales with its container.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="oDUK5gU03o" border="lg solid" border-sides="top" colspan="20"><h3>Media queries</h3><p>Rules change when the context changes.</p></grid-item></grid><p>Marcotte built on John Allsopp’s argument that print’s control is a function of the printed page’s limitation. The artifact becomes a set of relationships that resolves differently on every device.</p><h3>What you approve is a behaviour, not an image.</h3><p role="caption" class="source">Sources: <a href="https://alistapart.com/article/responsive-web-design/">A List Apart</a> · <a href="https://ethanmarcotte.com/wrote/responsive-design-at-10/">Ethan Marcotte, “Responsive Design at 10”</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="_VUT9rqnep"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="L6vN6uj1mQ"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">OPENTYPE 1.8</p><h2>2016: type stops being a set of objects and becomes a space</h2><p class="measure">Apple, Google, Microsoft and Adobe announced variable fonts at ATypI Warsaw on 14 September 2016. John Hudson defined the format as a single font file that behaves like multiple fonts.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug" class="tick" style="--marker-gap: 0.6em;"><grid-item id="pKcLYeF_R2" colspan="20"><h3>One file</h3><p>Variation is packaged as a designed system.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="fQcI2g1dzv" colspan="20"><h3>Authored axes</h3><p>Up to <b>64,000 axes</b> are permitted. Interpolation is designed, not automatic stretching.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="5wdY9uoSUk" colspan="20"><h3>Selected instance</h3><p>Names such as Bold label coordinates; context can choose another point.</p></grid-item></grid><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><h3>This is Gerstner’s programme shipped as a file format: the designer delivers the space, and context picks the instance.</h3><p role="caption" class="source">Sources: <a href="https://opensource.googleblog.com/2016/09/introducing-opentype-font-variations.html">Google Open Source</a> · <a href="https://blog.typekit.com/2016/09/14/variable-fonts-a-new-kind-of-font-for-flexible-design/">Typekit</a> · <a href="https://blog.fontlab.com/2016/09/14/fontlab-opentype-variations/">FontLab</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="JSTM_4kvZV"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="If9-WsayRC"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">PART THREE</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><display size="lg">III</display><h1 class="measure">Computational intelligence: the model becomes the medium</h1><p role="caption" class="gloss">The specification becomes statistical, production becomes abundant and judgment becomes the scarce resource.</p><hr class="heavy"><spacer mode="fill"></spacer></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="OSElhTUGMt"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="cmdY57l0p9"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">THE STRUCTURAL BREAK</p><h2>What actually changed: the specification stopped being explicit</h2><grid divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="2bopTzB5Ry" background-color="surface" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">DETERMINISTIC SYSTEMS</p><h3>Grid · stylesheet · variable font</h3><p>You can read the rule, predict the output and expect the same conditions to produce the same result.</p><p class="claim">The designer specifies relationships explicitly.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="qBNQ81PFon" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">STATISTICAL SYSTEMS</p><h3>Generative model</h3><p>The system is fitted to prior images and text. Its rule is not inspectable, and the same input need not repeat.</p><p class="claim">The designer prompts, curates and rejects.</p></grid-item></grid><h3>Production capacity becomes nearly free. Judgment about which output is right becomes the bottleneck.</h3><p role="caption" class="gloss">This is a shift in the cost structure of making, not evidence that taste has been automated.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="C60P6E-A2D"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="J7tT5gbSpO"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">GENERATIVE RISK</p><h2>The new failure mode is plausibility, not error</h2><grid marker="icon" marker-size="large" marker-style="outline" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill" class="chips"><grid-item id="95x4QdjrhY" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/Qk7T09fnnRIuEMixB4Hf.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="cluster converging toward average center" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Several dots grouped together, symbolizing the concept of cluster.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>The centre of gravity</h4><p>A model reproduces the corpus average: competent, familiar and recognizably generic.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="rzTxHpuEj5" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/ob3YSsthN3BidRnZvYUS.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="approved document with subtle warning" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;An exclamation mark formed from a thick black shape, set against a clean white background, signifies a warning or alert, drawing attention to potential danger or important information.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>The meeting problem</h4><p>A print error can be pointed to. Merely unremarkable work is harder to argue against.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="iLAb74qTgX" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/J4JmasKgvXR4W4e3zoLedT.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="three connected ethical questions" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Battery with three-quarters filled, indicating partial power or energy level.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>The open problems</h4><p>Training-data provenance, homogenization of visual culture, and accountability for output nobody composed.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="O1g1G-eBRY" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/uy0fGStxpEptJoigSlhC.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="editor selecting one precise option" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black pen nib within a square, signifying the concept of editing or writing.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>The counter-move</h4><p>Brief against the average, bring material the corpus does not contain, and edit hard.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="nAQsQoma_q"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="ny0J8lVkSc"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">THE TEACHING FRAME</p><h2>Three regimes, one direction of travel</h2><table col-widths="[17,28,27,28]" header-row="true" header-col="true" borders="rows" striped="false"><tbody><tr><td><p></p></td><td><p>European metal-type print<br>(1455–1980s)</p></td><td><p>Digital<br>(1985–2016)</p></td><td><p>Computational<br>(2016– )</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Unit of work</p></td><td><p>The cast letter, the printed forme</p></td><td><p>The file, the stylesheet, the component</p></td><td><p>The prompt, the dataset, the model</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Designer delivers</p></td><td><p>A finished artifact</p></td><td><p>A rule that generates artifacts</p></td><td><p>An intent plus a selection</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Control lost to</p></td><td><p>To the press and the trades</p></td><td><p>To the device and the viewport</p></td><td><p>To the model’s statistical prior</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Becomes cheap</p></td><td><p>Copies</p></td><td><p>Production and revision</p></td><td><p>Composition itself</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Becomes scarce</p></td><td><p>Access to the means</p></td><td><p>Craft attention at scale</p></td><td background-color="accent"><p>Judgment, framing, and editing</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p class="claim">Read the bottom row across: every revolution relocates scarcity; none abolishes it.</p><p role="caption" class="source">The date ranges are European and industrial markers for this lecture’s argument, not claims about where each technique first appeared.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="Z26nVwIeei" card-margin-hidden="true"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="2HqYekn36t" class="plate" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/dlIyg-QpEEI24fReZuyq8.png" saved-media-id="afoqj3zept4f39g" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="documentary-feature" alt="Documentary reportage photograph presented as an honest archival-style reconstruction of a mid-1970s experimental typography workshop at MIT, designers gathered around an early computer terminal and projection apparatus while layered letterforms move across a dark screen, candid mid-distance standing-eye-level view, available room light, natural monochrome film grain, no posed faces, no logos or readable signage" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item><grid-item id="M-ZedpGn4M" class="prose" padding="36pt 48pt 24pt" colspan="36"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">A DESIGNER OF TRANSITIONS</p><h3>Muriel Cooper designed the transition itself, in the 1970s</h3><p>Cooper, 1925 to 1994, spent four decades at MIT, first in its Office of Publications and then as design director of the MIT Press.</p><p>She co-founded the Visible Language Workshop in the mid-1970s and became founding faculty of the Media Lab.</p><p>Across print and screen, she pursued tools and interfaces that gave designers <b>greater control and quicker feedback</b>, eventually aided by machine intelligence.</p><p>Her <i>Information Landscapes</i>, presented the year she died, treated typography as something that moves, scales and responds.</p><p class="claim">She closes the argument because she designed metaphors, tools and teaching for the transition itself.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="caption" class="source">Source: <a href="https://core.ac.uk/download/161459016.pdf">Muriel Cooper research archive</a> · Image: illustrative reconstruction</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="28T2i5LNCj"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="6aQ6kowj7I" padding-bottom="16pt"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">WHAT REMAINS YOUR JOB</p><h1 class="measure wide">Five centuries of this history have automated <b>execution</b> repeatedly and <b>decision</b> never.</h1><h3 role="subheading" class="measure">Each transition rewarded designers who learned the new medium’s grain instead of defending the previous medium’s finish.</h3><p class="measure">Know what your system generates, know what it cannot, and be able to say why this output and not the plausible one beside it.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="eyebrow">SEMINAR QUESTION</p><h3 class="measure wide">The 42-line Bible imitated the manuscript, the first web pages imitated the printed page, and generated images imitate photography and illustration. What is the native form of a computational medium, and who decides?</h3><hr class="heavy"></grid-item></grid></section>